  "full_response": "The SOLID principles are a set of design guidelines in software engineering that aim to make software designs more understandable, flexible, and maintainable. Each principle focuses on a particular aspect of object-oriented design and programming. Here is an overview of the SOLID principles:\n\n1. **Single Responsibility Principle (SRP):**\n   - A class should have only one reason to change, meaning it should have only one job or responsibility. By separating concerns, classes become simpler, easier to understand, and less prone to errors.\n\n2. **Open/Closed Principle (OCP):**\n   - Software entities (classes, modules, functions, etc.) should be open for extension but closed for modification. This means you can add new functionality without changing existing code, often by using inheritance or polymorphism to extend behaviors.\n\n3. **Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP):**\n   - Objects of a superclass should be replaceable with objects of a subclass without affecting the correctness of the program. In other words, subclasses should extend parent classes without changing their behavior. This ensures that a program utilizing references to base classes remains functional when derived classes are introduced.\n\n4. **Interface Segregation Principle (ISP):**\n   - Clients should not be forced to depend on interfaces they do not use. Instead of one large interface, multiple specific interfaces are preferred. This promotes decoupling by allowing clients to implement only the interfaces that are relevant to them.\n\n5. **Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP):**\n   - High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules. Both should depend on abstractions (e.g., interfaces). Additionally, abstractions should not depend on details; details should depend on abstractions. This principle helps to reduce the coupling between components, making the system more modular and easier to refactor or replace specific parts.\n\nOverall, the SOLID principles guide developers towards creating robust and scalable software architectures that can evolve with changing requirements.",
